@Tutorial(time: 20) {
    @Intro(title: "Turn Your Room Into an Aquarium") {
        Point the iPad camera at your actual room and float the aquarium
        in the space around you.

        > Important: This chapter needs a real device (iPad running
        > iPadOS 18 or later).

        @Image(source: "03-intro", alt: "An AR aquarium placed in a real room")
    }

    @Section(title: "Switch From a Virtual Camera to Real Space") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            So far RealityView has shown the scene through a virtual camera.
            Change the camera mode to `.spatialTracking` so that your own
            room becomes the background.

            @Image(source: "03-section1", alt: "A room seen through camera passthrough, with the world origin marked")
        }

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Go back to Xcode and open the RoomAquarium project settings.
                To use the camera you have to tell the user why and get their
                permission. In **Build Settings**, find the Info.plist Values
                list, look for `Privacy - Camera Usage Description`, and write
                "We use the camera to turn your room into an aquarium."

                If the entry isn't there, add it with the + button and then
                fill it in.

                @Image(source: "03-s1-step1", alt: "The camera permission string in Build Settings")
            }

            @Step {
                Until now you explored the scene by dragging and zooming with
                `.realityViewCameraControls(.orbit)`. From here on the iPad's
                camera and movement do that job, so the aquarium looks like it
                is really in the room. Delete that line and prepare a
                `SpatialTrackingSession`.

                A SpatialTrackingSession tracks where the device is in real
                space and how it moves. You need the same session for as long
                as the view is on screen, so keep it in `@State`.

                > Tip: Also change the scene-loading code from `try?` to
                > `do-catch`. On a real device it's hard to tell why the screen
                > is empty, so if loading fails, have Xcode's console print
                > `Failed to load Scene:` and the reason.

                @Code(name: "AquariumView.swift", file: "03-code-01.swift", previousFile: "01-code-04.swift")
            }

            @Step {
                Now use a camera that follows the real device instead of
                RealityKit's virtual camera. When you move the iPad, the
                camera inside RealityKit moves with it, so the seahorse looks
                like it is sitting in real space.

                @Code(name: "AquariumView.swift", file: "03-code-02.swift", previousFile: "03-code-01.swift")
            }

            @Step {
                Make the seahorse blend into the real space a little more
                naturally.

                You turn on two features here. `.occlusion` hides the seahorse
                when a real object is in front of it. `.shadow` gives the
                seahorse a shadow so it looks like it's really in the room.

                > Tip: `run` returns the features the device doesn't support.
                > Unsupported features are dropped without an error, so
                > printing them to the console tells you right away why the
                > seahorse isn't being hidden.

                @Code(name: "AquariumView.swift", file: "03-code-03.swift", previousFile: "03-code-02.swift")
            }

            @Step {
                Connect the iPad to your Mac and run against the device.
                Allow camera access and your room appears as the background,
                with the seahorse floating right there. Pick the device up
                and walk around it.

                > Note: If the app stops while running from Xcode and the
                > `Render` thread shows `__pthread_kill`, Xcode's Metal debug
                > check is the cause. Go to **Product > Scheme > Edit Scheme… >
                > Run > Diagnostics**, turn off **Metal API Validation**, and run
                > again. The check only runs when Xcode launches a debug build, so
                > apps installed from TestFlight or the App Store aren't affected.

                @Image(source: "03-s1-result", alt: "The aquarium floating in a room, running on a device")
            }
        }
    }

    @Section(title: "Interact With the Seahorse") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            Make the seahorse spin once and play a sound when you tap it.

            @Image(source: "03-section2", alt: "The seahorse reacting to a tap")
        }

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                In **Timelines** in the Editor Panel at the bottom, create a
                new timeline and name it `TapSeahorse`.

                @Image(source: "03-s2-step1", alt: "An empty timeline named TapSeahorse")
            }

            @Step {
                Drag **Emphasize** out of the action list and drop it in.

                @Image(source: "03-s2-step2", alt: "An Emphasize action placed on a timeline track")
            }

            @Step {
                Set Target to `Seahorse` and Motion Type to `Spin`.
                Set Duration to 1.

                @Image(source: "03-s2-step3", alt: "Target, Motion Type, and Duration settings for the Emphasize action")
            }

            @Step {
                On the track just below Emphasize, place a **Play Audio**
                action, set Target to `Seahorse`, and pick the `WhaleCry.usdz`
                audio you registered in Chapter 2. Set Duration to 1.

                They have to be on separate tracks to play at the **same
                time** — put them on one track and they play one after the
                other, so watch out.

                @Image(source: "03-s2-step4", alt: "Emphasize and Play Audio side by side on two tracks")
            }

            @Step {
                Now get the seahorse ready to receive the user's taps. Select
                `Seahorse` and add **Collision** and **Input Target** from
                **Add Component**.

                **Collision** creates the area used to decide whether the
                user's input touched the seahorse, and **Input Target** makes
                `Seahorse` something that can receive input.

                @Image(source: "03-s2-step5", alt: "Adding Collision and Input Target components to Seahorse")
            }

            @Step {
                Select `Seahorse` and add an On Tap trigger to its
                **Behaviors** component. Set the timeline to run to
                TapSeahorse, then save (Cmd+S).

                @Image(source: "03-s2-step6", alt: "An On Tap trigger wired to TapSeahorse")
            }

            @Step {
                Back to Xcode. Take the tap gesture and call
                applyTapForBehaviors() on the tapped entity — the seahorse.
                That call is what fires the On Tap Behavior you set up on it.

                Later you can change the Behavior's On Tap timeline in Reality
                Composer Pro and the seahorse behaves differently without
                opening Xcode at all.

                @Code(name: "AquariumView.swift", file: "03-code-04.swift", previousFile: "03-code-03.swift")
            }

            @Step {
                Run it and tap the seahorse. If it spins once while a whale
                call plays, it worked.

                If nothing happens, check the size of the Collision you added
                to Seahorse. If the Collision area is much smaller than the
                seahorse, taps may not register.

                @Image(source: "03-s2-result", alt: "The seahorse spinning in response to a tap")
            }
        }
    }
}
